= i
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Take metric signature $(+---)$ and the <Fourier transform> convention $e^{-ipx}$. Introduce an even source $J$ for the gauge field and odd sources $\eta,\bar\eta$ for the <Faddeev-Popov ghost field>:
$$
Z[J,\bar\eta,\eta]=\frac1{Z[0]}\int\mathcal DA\,\mathcal D\bar c\,\mathcal Dc\,\exp\left[iS+i\int d^4x\,(J^{a\mu}A_\mu^a+\bar\eta^ac^a+\bar c^a\eta^a)\right].
$$
At tree level the two-point functions come from the quadratic action. Integrating the gauge kinetic term by parts gives its momentum-space kernel
$$
K_{\mu\nu}^{ab}(p)=\delta^{ab}\left[-p^2g_{\mu\nu}+(1-\zeta^{-1})p_\mu p_\nu\right].
$$
Its inverse follows from transverse and longitudinal projectors: $K=-p^2(P_T+\zeta^{-1}P_L)$, so $K^{-1}=-p^{-2}(P_T+\zeta P_L)$. Completing the bosonic Gaussian square gives $Z_0[J]=\exp[-iJK_F^{-1}J/2]$, with the <Feynman i-epsilon prescription> in the inverse. Two source derivatives and their insertion factors give the <gauge-boson propagator> $D_F=iK_F^{-1}$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\langle\Omega|T A_\mu^a(x)A_\nu^b(y)|\Omega\rangle=\delta^{ab}\int\frac{d^4p}{(2\pi)^4}e^{-ip(x-y)}\frac{-i}{p^2+i0}\left[g_{\mu\nu}-(1-\zeta)\frac{p_\mu p_\nu}{p^2+i0}\right].}
$$
For $\zeta=1$ this reduces to the <Feynman-gauge adjoint propagator>. The longitudinal double pole is understood with the compatible Feynman boundary prescription.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The ghost kinetic term as printed is $-\partial^\mu\bar c^a\partial_\mu c^a$. Integration by parts gives $\bar c^a\Box c^a$, so its Fourier-space quadratic kernel is $K_c^{ab}(p)=-p^2\delta^{ab}$. Completing the Grassmann square in the same generating functional gives
$$
Z_0[\bar\eta,\eta]=\exp[-i\bar\eta K_{c,F}^{-1}\eta].
$$
Keep the source order fixed, using a left derivative for $\bar\eta$ and a right derivative for $\eta$. Including the two insertion factors produces $\langle Tc\bar c\rangle=iK_{c,F}^{-1}$, just as in the <Gaussian generating functional for a Dirac field>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\langle\Omega|T c^a(x)\bar c^b(y)|\Omega\rangle=\delta^{ab}\int\frac{d^4p}{(2\pi)^4}e^{-ip(x-y)}\frac{-i}{p^2+i0}.}
$$
The minus sign follows from the particular ghost Lagrangian in this paper. A convention with the opposite ghost kinetic sign has the opposite $c\bar c$ propagator; redefining the <Faddeev-Popov antighost field> changes its interaction and source signs at the same time. Mixing propagator and vertex conventions would give an erroneous loop sign. This is the <ghost propagator for a negative derivative kinetic term>.

= Solution
{parent=4}

Expanding the covariant derivative isolates the <ghost-gluon vertex> interaction
$$
\mathcal L_{\bar cAc}=-g f^{abc}(\partial^\mu\bar c^a)A_\mu^b c^c.
$$
The derivative acts on the antighost. With all momenta incoming, let its momentum be $k$, the gluon's Lorentz index be $\mu$, and their color indices be $(a,b,c)$ in the order $(\bar c,A,c)$. The convention $e^{-ikx}$ replaces $\partial^\mu$ by $-ik^\mu$, and multiplication by $i$ from $e^{iS}$ gives
$$
\boxed{V_{\bar c^aA_\mu^bc^c}=-g f^{abc}k_\mu.}
$$
Momentum conservation is implicit. The <structure constants> reflect the <adjoint representation>, and the antighost momentum follows directly from its differentiated field. Reversing the momentum convention changes the displayed vertex sign consistently; exchanging the ghost and antighost source order also carries a <fermionic sign>.
